                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "1b92b384-87d5-38e3-822f-d98869b2df9d",
                        "entry_id": "0fde8336-db31-3866-a411-4c06ee37b130",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To shape crudely; to form in its first rudiments, without correction or polish."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "d5fb6591-56a3-4b63-92d1-47ef984693a7",
                                "sense_id": "1b92b384-87d5-38e3-822f-d98869b2df9d",
                                "text": "This rough cast unhewn poetry was instead of stage plays for 120 years"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5a4a340c-c303-4c7f-b1bc-b90d20ded155",
                                "sense_id": "1b92b384-87d5-38e3-822f-d98869b2df9d",
                                "text": "Nor bodily, nor ghostly negro could / Rough-ccast thy figure in a sadder mould."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ba970b5b-7242-4d7f-af0c-5d9883067f72",
                                "sense_id": "1b92b384-87d5-38e3-822f-d98869b2df9d",
                                "text": "Dutt (1913) states that madraspatanum wasps build all their cells and then roughcast the whole group together, i.e. that they make a crepissage."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "56195ff7-0a2b-45d5-b5bc-87d688411a80",
                                "sense_id": "1b92b384-87d5-38e3-822f-d98869b2df9d",
                                "text": "In particular, it seeks to elucidate the five path system, which was roughcasted at the time of Abhidharma Buddhism and later improved in terms of more comprehensive definition and fruition by Mahayana Buddhism."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d7ae6227-ceb8-4a1a-a40e-6f752bd34d70",
                                "sense_id": "1b92b384-87d5-38e3-822f-d98869b2df9d",
                                "text": "However, although steam-powered machines were used in the 19th century also to roughcast and polish mass-produced lenses for everyday use of just a few centimeters in diameter (for glasses and lorgnettes, opera glasses and small cheap telescopes), those of the best quality, used mainly in optical equipment, were still manufactured and finished off by hand, one by one."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a43c861d-1190-3f79-91ff-20204ce9c8d6",
                        "entry_id": "0fde8336-db31-3866-a411-4c06ee37b130",
